For purposes of this chapter, the following terms shall have the following meanings.
Dwelling means one or more rooms designed and occupied as living quarters of a family, individual, or group of individuals. Dwellings include single-family detached dwellings and multifamily dwelling units.
Noise means any sound audible to the human ear. Noise includes but is not limited to:
(1) Sound produced by mechanical sound-making devices such as radios, stereos, boom boxes, portable media players, televisions, musical instruments, horns, whistles, bells, chimes, loudspeakers, sound amplifiers and other machines and devices for the producing, reproducing or amplifying of sound;
(2) Human-produced sounds such as yelling, shouting, hooting, whistling, singing, speaking and arguing;
(3) Commercial advertising sounds;
(4) Party noise;
(5) Sound coming from motorized landscape maintenance devices such as lawn mowers, edgers, weed-whackers, leaf blowers, and chain saws;
(6) Animal vocalizations from pets or non-domesticated animals;
(7) Burglar, fire or car alarms;
(8) Noise coming from construction and demolition activities such as hammering, nailing, drilling, sawing and paving; and
(9) Noise coming from any commercial property or commercial vehicle.
Plainly audible means any noise which can be heard or detected by the unaided and unimpaired human ear. Words and phrases need not be discernable in order for them to be considered plainly audible.
Residential area means any area zoned for residential use.
Sealed dwelling means any dwelling that has all of its windows and doors closed.
Unreasonably loud means annoying or disturbing to the peace, quiet and comfort of a reasonable person of ordinary sensitivities.
(Ord. No. 24-05, § 2(§ 3), 11-14-2024)

